      "text": "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin is of the form \\(\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\\).\n\nGiven that the ellipse touches the x-axis at two points, therefore, \\(b = 1\\), and the center of the ellipse is initially at the origin.\n\nThe distance between the center and each focus is \\(c\\), where \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\). In this case, \\(c = \\sqrt{7 - 1} = \\sqrt{6}\\).\n\nThe distance between the foci is \\(2c = 2\\sqrt{6}\\).\n\nIn this case, the foci are \\((\\sqrt{6}, 0)\\) and \\((- \\sqrt{6}, 0)\\).\nTo solve the problem, we need to determine the coordinates of the foci of the ellipse. The given ellipse touches the x-axis at two points, meaning that the maximum value of \\(y\\) is 1. Since the ellipse touches the x-axis, this means that the point of tangency is at the top of the ellipse, where \\(y = 1\\). Therefore, \\(b = 1\\).\n\nThe standard form of the ellipse equation is \\(\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\\). Since the ellipse touches the x-axis at \\(y = 1\\), we have \\(\\frac{1}{1^2} = 1\\). This implies that \\(a = 2\\). This is because the barycenter of the ellipse (which is the center in this case) is at \\(x = 1\\) and the semi-major axis is \\(a = 2\\).\n\nThe distance between the foci of the ellipse is given by \\(2c\\), where \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\). Plugging in the values, we get:\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{2^2 - 1^2} = \\sqrt{4 - 1} = \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the foci is:\n\n\\[\n2c = 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the answer is \\(\\boxed{2\\sqrt{6}}\\).",
